  "final": "The shared export path is in place. `export_csv` and `export_json` return the same strings as before. `python3 checks/public.py` and my new `python3 checks/test_exporter.py` both pass.\n\n- **`src/exporter.py` (new):** `export_rows(rows, format)` handles `'csv'` and `'json'` and raises `ValueError(\"unsupported format\")` for anything else. A single normalization step keeps only `id` and `name`, uses `''` for missing or `None` values, and stringifies the rest. It builds new dicts in order, so it doesn't mutate the input and works on one-shot iterables. The CSV and JSON settings are the ones you specified, and the header is written even with no rows.\n- **`src/csv_exporter.py` and `src/json_exporter.py`:** each is now a one-line wrapper that calls `export_rows`. Their own normalization and serialization loops are gone.\n- **`src/dispatcher.py`:** I left it unchanged. It still calls the two wrappers, so it goes through the shared path and keeps its existing `ValueError`.\n- **`checks/test_exporter.py` (new):** covers these cases:\n  - quoting and non-ASCII output\n  - missing and `None` values\n  - extra keys being dropped\n  - input not being mutated\n  - iterator input\n  - empty input\n  - the dispatcher\n  - unsupported formats, including `None` and unhashable values\n\nI also compared the new wrappers against a copy of the old implementations on a few awkward rows (newlines, quotes, non-ASCII, `None`, `0`, an empty dict), and the output was identical. The workspace has no git, so I couldn't diff against the original files.",
